  "summary": "Recent simulation preprints and a review link deleterious variation to genetic load and breeding gain.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how the distribution of fitness-relevant variation, particularly deleterious alleles, constrains evolutionary and applied outcomes. A review of deleterious mutations covers identification strategies, cross-species variation, evolutionary persistence, and implications for crop breeding and conservation. Forward simulations report that reproductive competition amplifies purifying selection and reduces genetic load more efficiently than the comparison scenario. Individual-based mosquito models show that standing genetic variation evolves under a homing suppression gene drive, altering drive conversion and resistance outcomes. In admixed crop germplasm, simulations show that low founder isoeliteness reduces genomic selection gain, and an isoeliteness-guided method is proposed to address this. The gene drive and genomic selection items extend the thread beyond load alone, so it describes the dominant theme rather than every item. Most evidence is simulation-based or preprint-stage.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
